sam (docs): Heads-up — found a C# quickstart snippet that inlines the API key and uses .Result on an async call. This will both encourage bad creds placement and can deadlock in ASP.NET apps.

sam (docs): Problematic snippet I saw: ```csharp
// BAD: hardcoding key + blocking on task
var client = new RedwoodClient("sk-xxxxx");
var response = client.GenerateAsync(request).Result;
Console.WriteLine(response.Text);
```

maya (devex): Oof. :facepalm: We should standardize on an env-var factory method and await everywhere. Also mention ConfigureAwait(false) for library authors.

igor (sdk): I can send a tiny PR. Proposed replacement: ```csharp
// GOOD: read from env + async/await
var client = RedwoodClient.FromEnvironment();
var response = await client.GenerateAsync(request);
Console.WriteLine(response.Text);
```

igor (sdk): Will add a short comment about token rotation and not storing keys in source.

sam (docs): Great — while you're at it, please update the quickstart snippet path and the onboarding gist. The README currently links to quickstart/csharp/README.md which still has the old example.

maya (devex): Also add a small note under "Auth" that recommends using a secret manager or env vars and shows dotnet user-secrets for local dev.

sam (docs): I'll open an issue and tag the PR. Thanks.

